On the central cross-application dashboard, please make the following fixes and enhancements to the Resource Utilization widget, its refresh behavior, and its link to the underlying job.

1. Refresh, applied globally: the manual refresh button currently only refreshes the Pod Count Summary widget, not the Resource Utilization widget. Fix that. Also check whether Resource Utilization already participates in the dashboard's existing auto-refresh timer, and if not, wire it in. Going forward, treat this as a global rule: every widget on this dashboard, current or future, must respond to both the manual refresh button and the auto-refresh timer, with no widget-specific exceptions.

2. Popover standardization: the hover tooltip on the Resource Utilization chart is rendering as a plain default white tooltip instead of the dark, rounded-corner popover component already built as a shared/reusable component elsewhere in the app. Switch it to use that existing shared component instead of any local/default tooltip.

3. Toggle instead of extra charts: do not add a second chart or a second row per application, that is too much information. Instead add a single toggle control on the widget with two states, Limits and Current Utilization. In Limits mode (today's default), each application's chart shows reserved/limit CPU versus total tenant quota. In Current Utilization mode, the same chart instead shows actual/live CPU utilization versus total tenant quota. The chart label and the popover content must update to reflect whichever mode is currently selected. The widget keeps its existing one-chart-per-application footprint; no resizing needed for this.

4. Chart enrichment: the current donut/pie chart is small and shows little beyond a bare ring. Make it richer, for example a clear center label with the key number or percentage, a small legend or label describing the segments, and a more live feel such as a smooth transition when values update on refresh. Also extend the popover to show a per-namespace CPU-used-versus-that-namespace's-quota breakdown, matching the detail already available in the full downloadable report, on top of whichever tenant-level total (limit or current) is active.

5. Drill-down to the job: this widget's chart for a given application is fed by a tenant utilization job that runs in the Jobs/Console tab. Clicking an application's chart should navigate to that job's console output tab for that application.

6. In-memory output retention and two-way sync: no job-history logging is needed for this job, so leave history off. Instead, keep the job's last console output in memory and keep showing it when the user navigates back to that job's tab, instead of it disappearing once they leave an active run. Track this retention against the widget's refresh interval, default 15 minutes, keeping it available for up to about an hour, and refresh it whenever the dashboard's auto-refresh or manual refresh triggers a new run for that application. Also make this work the other way: if the user manually runs this job directly from the Jobs/Console tab, the corresponding Resource Utilization widget entry for that application should update too, so the widget and the job's output always stay in sync regardless of which side triggered the run.

7. Generalize the pattern: this widget-to-job correlation and sync behavior should be built as a reusable pattern for any future job-backed widget, not special-cased to this one.

Keep all existing working behavior intact (parallel per-application refresh, existing job locking, existing widget data persistence across refresh/navigation) while making these changes, and confirm which existing shared components you are reusing before adding new ones.
